    client: "NHS",
    description: "Ribbon",
    overview:
      "Choreographer Supple Nam Brings Heartfelt Dance to NHS Bowel Cancer Awareness Ad.\nWhen the director duo Si&Ad and producer Georgina Smith approached choreographer Supple Nam with a challenging brief for an NHS ad, to raise awareness for bowel cancer screening in a way that’s impactful, yet uplifting. The concept was both exciting and meaningful, with the potential to combine fun, dance, and an important message about health.\nTurning Toilet Paper into a Dance of Hope:\nThe script was unique—using a simple roll of toilet paper to tell a profound story. The challenge? To transform something as everyday as toilet paper into a symbol of hope and health awareness. While Si&Ad initially considered CGI, Supple’s enthusiasm led him to explore using real toilet rolls, different props, and materials to create a ribbon-like dance. The concept: a sad story with a hopeful ending, encouraging people to take responsibility for their health before it’s too late.\nThe idea of using real toilet paper for the dance worked beautifully, adding an element of authenticity and fun. Over several days, Supple and the directors worked to refine the choreography, developing moves that were not only entertaining but also emotionally resonant. By the time they reached the casting day, the agency and client were excited to see their vision coming to life.\nA Dance That Speaks to the Heart:\nSupple's mission was clear: make the choreography relatable, inspiring, and engaging. Through research and experimentation with the toilet roll as a prop, he sought to find movements that would connect with audiences, spark joy, and emphasize the importance of taking action for one’s health.\nSean August Lewis was cast as the lead, and his natural energy and ability to embody the choreography made him the perfect choice. His dynamic performance captured the spirit of the piece, beautifully telling the story through dance.\nPassion and Dedication on Set:\nWith just two and a half days of rehearsal in the studio, plus additional time on location, the entire team poured their passion into the shoot. Supple’s direction, combined with the enthusiasm of the cast and crew, ensured that every movement was filled with heart, energy, and purpose. The result? A memorable and impactful ad that combines creativity with a crucial health message.\n",
