    client: "Ferrari",
    description: "Mission Ferrari",
    overview:
      "When agency Business Creative (UK) and Ferrari World theme park in Abu Dhabi decided on commissioning a truly unique show that would wow visitors, they re-approach award-winning choreographer and Director, Supple Nam to choreograph and direct an aerial action stunt show on a wall. Recognised across the world as a true visionary Supple Nam went to work with the assistance of the projection mapping company Drive to research and prepare his ideas.\nOnce Supple's concept and ideas had been signed off by the client, his casting team and selected management from Ferrari World, went straight into searching for aerialists, dancers, acrobats and martial artists, across Europe, UK and America. Through the selection process Supple workshopped and auditioned applicants where he could and if not, would request casting tapes. \nAfter a lengthy process Supple ended up going with 2 aerialists from New York and a stunt “tricker” from London. With 10 days scheduled for R&D, Supple hired 2 of the UK’s most repeatable aerialists Simon Fee and Alex Poulter. Together, they worked out different techniques to help Supple map out the entire show. Working closely with the animators, music composer, aerialist specialist’s, harness programmer, production and client, Supple tackled as much as he could before taking the show to the rehearsal stage, on location in Abu Dhabi.\nWith an intense 6 week rehearsal scheduled in Abu Dhabi, Supple and his aerial specialist Simon Fee, on arrival found out the wall was not ready and with no exact time when it would be, Supple and his stunt team kept training and working on choreography to be as prepared as they could to go on the 18m tall x 8m wide vertical wall weighing over 18 tonnes. Once allowed to go on the wall the 6 weeks rehearsals began but were pressured to try to make up for the lost time. With safety measures and technical glitches such as the harness reprogram to be programmed again and adjusted, and making sure safety was kept an eye on all times. Supple and his team finally finished creating, choreographing and directing the show.\nWith over 80,000 people watching the show, and achieving national and international coverage. Critics from The National, Arts & Lifestyle, called the shows ‘spectacular’ and ‘thrilling’ and The Huffington Post which announced that it was the launch of a ‘brand new kind of theatre’.\nFerrari World extended the run of Mission Ferrari for another 3 months. Talks of making action figures for the characters in Mission Ferrari. Everyone was very excited especially when Mission Ferrari won the 2015 Brass Spring Award for “Best Sports Show Performance Act”.",
